**Presenter:** Xuan Liu  
**Co-author:** Kunio Shirahada  
**Session:** Policy Levers for Circular Economy (2-3D-2)

How can public authorities oversee circular economy practices while giving firms enough discretion to adjust their day-to-day operations? This talk explores that question through two eco-industrial parks in Jiangsu Province, China. The study draws on interviews, site observations, and policy documents to compare a state-owned petrochemical enterprise in Nanjing with two privately owned aluminum-processing SMEs in Jiangyin.

The cases illustrate two ways digital verification supports coordination. In Nanjing, environmental, safety, and production monitoring helped make emerging operational risks visible and supported timely corrective action. In Jiangyin, production, material traceability, and certification records helped public authorities understand what firms were already doing and where targeted support could help.

The talk describes digital verification as a **governance hack** because it puts existing records and monitoring systems to a new use. Public authorities can check implementation and follow up, while firms use their technical knowledge to decide how to respond. Shared evidence makes it possible to identify problems, adjust operations, and verify the results in subsequent rounds. This process helps explain how circular practices can be sustained under ongoing regulatory oversight.

